menuconfig ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG
    bool "Runtime-adjustable hold-tap and debounce timing (torabo-tsuki)"
    help
      Make the numbers that decide how a key FEELS editable at runtime instead of
      at build time: the hold-tap parameters of the two stock nodes &mt (mod_tap)
      and &lt (layer_tap), and the matrix kscan debounce windows.

      Tuning these is trial and error — a tapping term is either right for your
      hands or it isn't, and you only find out by typing — so a reflash per
      attempt is the wrong loop. This makes it a slider.

      Needs the matching hooks in the zmk fork (<zmk/torabo_timing.h>). Those are
      __weak and default to stock behavior, so a fork without this module built
      in behaves exactly as before; this module supplies the strong definitions.

      Enable on the CENTRAL: this is the half the app talks to, and hold-taps are
      decided there. The debounce windows reach the other half too, if both are
      built with ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_DEBOUNCE_SYNC and the peripheral with
      ZMK_TIMING_SPLIT_PERIPHERAL (the torabo-timing-split snippet).

if ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG

module = ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG

# BLE detail (was CONFIG_ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG_BLE's own help; kept here since
# Kconfig help text can't take a $(var) — see
# features/common/Kconfig.template.torabo_ble):
#   Custom encrypted GATT service (e1f4b000) to read/write the settings live.
#   The wire is a fixed 96 bytes: it fits a single ATT write once the MTU has
#   been negotiated up (the torabo-combos / torabo-trackball snippets already
#   raise it), and the write path reassembles chunks when it has not.
torabo-thing = "the timing settings"
rsource "../common/Kconfig.template.torabo_ble"

# TUNNEL detail (was CONFIG_ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG_TUNNEL's own help tail):
#   Carries the exact same 96-byte wire blob as the GATT service (feature id
#   0x10), and coexists with it.
torabo-thing = "the timing settings"
rsource "../common/Kconfig.template.torabo_tunnel"

config ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG_DEBOUNCE_PRESS_MS
    int "Devicetree debounce-press-ms of the matrix kscan"
    default 5
    range 1 100
    help
      What the kscan node's debounce-press-ms actually is in this build. The
      driver's devicetree config is private to it, so there is no way to read the
      value back; this is how a READ can answer with the true default before the
      user has written anything, and what "reset to default" in the app means.

      KEEP IN SYNC with the shield's kscan node. Getting it wrong costs nothing
      at runtime (the firmware still runs on the real devicetree value until a
      write happens) but the app will show the wrong starting number.

      ⚠ C-6 (docs/COMPATIBILITY.md, PLAN-ext-fw-refactor.md phase 7): this is a
      manual copy of the shield overlay's kscan debounce-press-ms, kept in sync
      by hand — there is no build-time check the two agree, and (unlike
      TORABO_RESERVED_LAYERS / C-5) this value never reaches torabo_caps, so a
      mismatch is invisible until someone reads the "current" value in the app
      and compares it to the overlay by eye.

config ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG_DEBOUNCE_RELEASE_MS
    int "Devicetree debounce-release-ms of the matrix kscan"
    default 5
    range 1 100
    help
      See ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG_DEBOUNCE_PRESS_MS.

module-str = ZMK Timing Config
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG

menuconfig ZMK_TIMING_SPLIT_PERIPHERAL
    bool "Apply the debounce windows pushed by the split central (peripheral)"
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT && !ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    depends on !ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG
    help
      The peripheral half of the debounce settings. Each half scans its own
      matrix, so a value the app writes to the central would otherwise stop
      there; this takes the two bytes the central pushes over the split link and
      applies them to this half's kscan from the next scan.

      Needs CONFIG_ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_DEBOUNCE_SYNC on BOTH halves — that is the zmk
      fork symbol that adds the characteristic here and the writer there. The
      torabo-timing-split snippet sets both.

      Nothing is stored: the central re-sends on every connect, so there is one
      source of truth. For the second or two before the link is up, this half
      scans on its devicetree values.

      Deliberately NOT for the central — ZMK_TIMING_CONFIG owns the same seam
      (zmk_torabo_debounce_effective) there, with the wire and the NVS store
      behind it.

if ZMK_TIMING_SPLIT_PERIPHERAL

module = ZMK_TIMING_SPLIT_PERIPHERAL
module-str = ZMK Timing Split Peripheral
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # ZMK_TIMING_SPLIT_PERIPHERAL
